#usda 1.0
(
    defaultPrim = "DriveLaw"
    upAxis = "Y"
    metersPerUnit = 1
)

# Modelica DRIVE LAW for a skid rover — the `driveLaw = "modelica"` variant
# references this into the rover ROOT, exactly as the `drivetrain` variants
# reference their components: a variant CHOOSES a component, it never restates
# one. Everything below composes onto the rover; `</DriveLaw.…>` paths
# translate to the HOST rover root through the reference arc.
#
# What it does: replaces the instant built-in skid mixing with a physical
# command→torque law — `models/LunCo/Mobility/RoverDrivetrain.mo` integrates a first-order
# motor lag per side on the solver clock. Native USD connections project the
# outputs onto the rover ports; no tick script participates.
#
# NOT `lunco:spawnable` — an overlay for a rover that already has FSW ports.
# ── The law FORWARDS the vessel's output; it does not write an input ─────────
#
# `drive_left` on a rover root is an OUTPUT — the mixed command every wheel
# consumes (`inputs:demand.connect = </SkidRover.outputs:drive_left>`). A drive
# law replaces the PRODUCER of that signal, so the connection to author is an
# OUTPUT-TO-OUTPUT forward: the root's output resolves to the law's output.
#
#     float outputs:drive_left.connect = </DriveLaw/Drivetrain.outputs:drive_left>
#
# This file used to author `inputs:drive_left.connect` instead, which cannot
# work: a connection names a consumer's input and a producer's output, and there
# is no `inputs:` half of `drive_left` on the root to write into. The measured
# result was a rover that travelled 0.00 m while its control reached 2.13 m/s —
# `apply_drive_mix` stood the built-in down exactly as asked and nothing took its
# place. The same forwarding idiom is already used by `skid_rover.usda`'s own
# network root with an explicit component collection and member connections.
#
# ── Why the model stays in a CHILD prim ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Not for tidiness — the rover root is already taken. It references
# `components/avionics/obc.usda`, so it ALREADY carries `LunCoProgramAPI` and an
# `info:sourceAsset`. That attribute is single-valued and the OBC's arc is
# stronger, so a second `info:sourceAsset` here would be silently overridden and
# the drive law would never bind at all. One prim, one model.
#
# `derive_usd_connections` materialises these forwards onto the vessel's actuator
# ports. The production parity fixture proves the Modelica law owns the command:
# it retains the authored first-order lag while converging to the built-in
# kernel's terminal speed.
def Xform "DriveLaw" (
    kind = "component"
)
{
    # These connected outputs are the authoritative allocation. Their presence
    # prevents derivation of a second, imperative DriveMix on the vessel.
    float outputs:drive_left.connect = </DriveLaw/Drivetrain.outputs:drive_left>
    float outputs:drive_right.connect = </DriveLaw/Drivetrain.outputs:drive_right>

    # The physics: per-side first-order motor lag, solver-clocked.
    def Scope "Drivetrain" (
        prepend apiSchemas = ["LunCoProgramAPI"]
    )
    {
        uniform asset info:sourceAsset = @lunco://models/LunCo/Mobility/RoverDrivetrain.mo@
        # `</DriveLaw.…>` = the host rover root after arc translation: the
        # model reads the same FSW command ports the built-in kernels read.
        float inputs:throttle.connect = </DriveLaw.inputs:throttle>
        float inputs:steer.connect = </DriveLaw.inputs:steer>
    }
}
